\f1\fs22 Chanta delivered his scrawled lyrics to the High Elvis in the green room of the night\'92s headline act, The High Fidelities, never once wondering if the name was a clone joke. The High Elvis scanned the pad, nodded and firmly pat Chanta\'92s shoulder in acknowledgment. \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The band went to work. They went at it with tremendous conviction, over and over, moaning and la-la-ing down blind alleys. They went at it flat, and sharp; they jumped audacious intervals in an attempt to avoid writing something safe and therefore probably already in the repertoire of Sir Paul McCartney; they experimented with rhythms in which the lyrics could never scan. They hummed parts they hadn\'92t ruined yet. No one could have told you the moment it turned. Only that a breath before, they\'92d been idiots, and then they weren\'92t, and the tune had been smugly sitting among them the whole time, waiting for them to stop embarrassing themselves. In the end, it was the kind of tune Sir Paul might have written, if only he was American.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The beams of small flashlights traced through the black as stage crew guided musicians to their positions. The noise of the crowd rose from excited anticipation to something closer to pouring a cargo-ship-load of wing nuts down the world\'92s largest water park slide. Four tight spots flashed to full and produced the High Elvis on centre stage as if they\'92d teleported him there. A beat later, the band hit their cues and launched a fast rockabilly tune that few of any of the less mutant universes could play as well.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crowd became excited bacteria in a bloodstream within three beats of music from the half-million-watt sound system aimed at them. Nothing would contain their energy. Those that knew how (mostly Elvi) jived, strolled or boogie-woogied, while the rest shook like ecstatic pentecostalists with rubber legs. The Airborne kids threw their air guitars in the air like they just didn\'92t care, and failed to catch most of them. On the VIP platform, Sarah jumped to her feet and danced as best she could, feeling somehow aided by an energy that flowed from Little Clinton through her limbs and out to the crowd. Kathy danced like a gym instructor in a tie-dyed lab-coat with a traffickable quantity of  enhancements in her system. Hayley tried to mimic Kathy but mostly just tapped her toes and shook her hands. Eloise\'97Eloise danced like she was just an arms dealer in a rock\'92n\'92roll band and sold it with ease. Those of her competitors who caught glimpses of her on the livestream were even more terrified of her than before.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The first song ended with a blare of brass and a frenzied trash-can assault on the drum kit. A moment of silence followed a moment of echo, and then the crowd erupted in cheers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The centre-front stage lit back up, and everyone saw the High Elvis had unexpected company. Three pairs of scuffed medieval boots, three sets of robes depicting arcane symbols and washing instructions, three tapered beards that were older than anyone in the audience, including Eloise and one of her entourage, three wizard hats (one with a pompom) and three expressions, one each of patience, compassion and amusement. Oh, and three ukuleles.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>the High Elvis covered the mic. \'93What the tax are you doing here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just a little public service announcement,\'94 the middle one said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Overhead, the Colonel turned on his tether and puffed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>the High Elvis inclined his head. It was not permission, exactly\'97more the nod of a man moving an item down the running order to make room for a thing that had, regrettably, always been on it. Still at the mic he announced, \'93The Three Sages of the Travelator, everyone!\'94 and applauded, because he figured on existing the crowd rathe than leave a gaping hole near the end of the night.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An engineer from the tower called down to the stage manager and asked if the Sages were wired for sound.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A bewildered stage manager answered, \'93Ah yeah, already done\'85 dunno when, hours ago I\'92d guess, three wireless mics and three wireless transmitters plugged into the ukes,\'94 and have the engineer the patch numbers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good century, everyone!\'94 Kleo called to the crowd and the crowd cheered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo started strumming a chord pattern, Solo punched in a riff after eight bars, and Kleo embroidered arpeggios over the top another eight later. With the song\'92s theme in full flight the three leapt about the stage like three Rumpelstiltskins in red shoes until they pulled the energy back to sing:\
