\f1\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Let me hear just the front!\'94 Solo called, and swept a pointed finger over the audience whose features he could make out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The front sang.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now the back,\'94 Chilo called next, saluting past the engineering tower.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The back sang. Two lines in, Chilo put hand to his ear, a chided, \'93I can\'92t hear you!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The back sang louder.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And so it went; the Sages called on a section of the crowd and the section sang\'97just the left, just the right, the Airborne, the Cloudborne. When called, the Elvis sang louder that their numbers might have promised, and the warmth of the voices was almost transcendent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo called for one more round from the Elvi while the Sages stopped playing. The sound of thousands of Elvi singing made crystal of the air which resonated to such notes of purity, the crowd was reduced to wonder. As the Elvi sang round after round, each quieter and sweeter than the last, the tune became more hymn than ditty. Many of the crowd were reduced to tears, even among the Airborne kids playing the markets or running books on the punch-ups.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi simplified the song, repeating two lines over until they let the refrain expire.\
