\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::2><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 While the Elvi continued, the jumbotrons flashed alive and beamed the light of a live transmission: Sarah was looking at herself: ten metres of her own face beaming from every screen around the lawn, the Holy Momma delivered at last to every part of the crowd.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise put hand over Sarah\'92s to stop her from trembling. Sarah took a deep, nervous breath and   conceded to herself, there were times when a girl can really use an arms-dealing mother-in-law.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the last Elvi note released a silent arena, Kleo rocked back on his sandals as though a wind had come up off the lawn. He laid a flat hand to his beard as the other two began strumming fro their ends of the stage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And then Kleo took a solo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What followed was, by any technical measure, extraordinary, full of hammers and pulls that would make a Los Angeles Guitar Academy graduate proud on a shredding guitar, let alone a uke. Amused at first, then tired of it after 32 bars, the audience stood like a congregation that had been asked to admire their vicar\'92s caravan. Kleo played on into the great baffled quiet, immaculate and unloved. Even the fig trees wanted to be anywhere else, and the air guitars had fallen silent.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 the High Elvis had seen more than a few solo hogs in his time. Once they had crossed a line, which they prevalently did, there was only one option. He primed his band in the dark of the stage and pulled Kleo\'92s plug. Within a heartbeat, his brass and rhythm sections launched and went on with the program.
